claimed: intent-name-sensitivity — the domain-in-names result rests on four identifiers, so ablate them. Four taxonomies differing ONLY in case names, run with no examples and no gate so the names are the sole signal: generic (Proposal/Request/...), the true domain (Meeting...), a WRONG domain (Shipping...), and a nonsense qualifier (Zarnic...). Three outcomes are distinguishable and all three are worth knowing. If the wrong domain pushes meeting messages into NotAboutShipping, the model READS the domain word rather than treating a qualifier as decoration. If the nonsense qualifier lifts Other the way Meeting did, what helps is that the names look chosen, not what they name — and the recommendation shipped last lane is weaker than it sounds. If only the true domain helps, the claim stands as written.
